          "arguments": {
            "content": "# Analysis of Statute of Limitations for Company A's Claim Against Company B\n\n## Case Summary\n\nDuring the Paris Olympics, Company A in China sold a batch of sports equipment to domestic Company B for use in Company B's export business. The parties agreed that Company B should pay for the goods within 30 days after delivery. However, after receiving the goods, Company B never made payment.\n\nIn August of the same year, Company B confirmed by email that it still owed RMB 500,000 in payment and promised to pay as soon as possible.\n\nAfter that, the parties engaged in multiple rounds of collection and negotiation, but the debt remained unpaid.\n\nIn September of the same year, a lawyer is preparing to file a lawsuit on behalf of Company A to recover the payment.\n\n**Question:** What is the statute of limitations for this claim?\n\n---\n\n## Legal Analysis\n\n### 1. Nature of the Legal Relationship\n\nThis case involves a **domestic sales contract** between Company A (seller) and Company B (buyer) within China. The key characteristics are:\n- **Contract type:** Sales contract (买卖合同)\n- **Parties:** Both companies are domestic Chinese entities\n- **Subject matter:** Sports equipment\n- **Payment obligation:** Company B agreed to pay within 30 days after delivery\n- **Breach:** Company B failed to pay after receiving the goods\n\n### 2. Applicable Legal Provisions\n\nAfter reviewing the legal materials in the repository, the relevant provisions for determining the statute of limitations are found in **Law 4** (中华人民共和国民法典 / Civil Code of the People's Republic of China).\n\n#### Key Provisions from the Civil Code:\n\n**Article 188 (General Limitation Period)**\n> \"The limitation period for filing a lawsuit with the People's Court to protect civil rights is **three years**. Where the law provides otherwise, such provisions shall prevail. The limitation period commences from the date when the right holder knows or should have known that his rights have been infringed upon and the obligor.\"\n\n**Article 195 (Interruption of Limitation Period)**\n> The limitation period is interrupted under any of the following circumstances, and the limitation period shall be recalculated from the time of interruption or the conclusion of the relevant proceedings:\n> 1. The right holder requests the obligor to perform the obligation;\n> 2. The obligor agrees to perform the obligation;\n> 3. The right holder files a lawsuit or applies for arbitration;\n> 4. Other circumstances that have the same effect as filing a lawsuit or applying for arbitration.\n\n**Article 196 (Exceptions - Non-Application of Limitation)**\n> The following claims shall not be subject to the limitation period:\n> 1. Claims for cessation of infringement, removal of obstacles, or elimination of dangers;\n> 2. Claims by the holders of real property rights or registered movable property rights for the return of property;\n> 3. Claims for payment of child support, alimony, or maintenance;\n> 4. Other claims that are not subject to the limitation period by law.\n\n**Article 197 (Non-Waiver of Limitation Rights)**\n> The period of limitation, methods of calculation, and reasons for suspension and interruption are prescribed by law, and the agreement of the parties is invalid. The parties' waiver of the benefits of the limitation period is invalid.\n\n**Article 198 (Special Provisions)**\n> Where special laws provide for the limitation period, such provisions shall prevail.\n\n**Article 199 (Period for Exercising Rights of Revocation or Termination)**\n> The period for exercising rights of revocation or termination as prescribed by law or agreed upon by the parties, unless otherwise provided by law, shall commence from the date when the right holder knows or should have known the reason for the formation of the right. If the right holder fails to exercise the right within the prescribed period after the period expires, the People's Court shall not protect it. The provisions on suspension, interruption, and extension of the limitation period do not apply to the period for exercising rights of revocation or termination.\n\n### 3. Application to This Case\n\n#### (1) Base Limitation Period: **3 Years**\n\nUnder **Article 188 of the Civil Code**, the general limitation period for contract disputes is **three years**.\n\n#### (2) Starting Point of the Limitation Period\n\nThe limitation period begins from the date when Company A **knew or should have known** that its rights were infringed and who the obligor is.\n\nIn this case:\n- The parties agreed that payment should be made **within 30 days after delivery**\n- Company B failed to pay within this period\n- Therefore, the limitation period **commences from the day after the 30-day payment period expired** (i.e., from the date when the payment obligation became due and Company B defaulted)\n\n#### (3) Interruption of the Limitation Period\n\nUnder **Article 195**, the limitation period is **interrupted** when:\n\n**First Interruption - August Email Confirmation:**\nCompany B confirmed by email in August that it owed RMB 500,000 and promised to pay as soon as possible. This constitutes the **obligor agreeing to perform the obligation** (Article 195(2)), which **interrupts** the limitation period. The 3-year clock restarts from the date of this acknowledgment.\n\n**Second Interruption - Multiple Collection and Negotiation Rounds:**\nThe subsequent \"multiple rounds of collection and negotiation\" between the parties constitute the **right holder requesting the obligor to perform** (Article 195(1)), which further **interrupts** the limitation period each time such a request is made.\n\n### 4. Critical Determination: The Statute of Limitations Has NOT Expired\n\nGiven the facts:\n\n1. **Original limitation period:** 3 years from the date of default (30 days after delivery)\n2. **August email acknowledgment:** Interrupts the limitation period, restarting the 3-year clock from August\n3. **Subsequent negotiations:** Each collection effort and negotiation further interrupts the limitation period\n4. **September lawsuit preparation:** The lawyer is preparing to file in September of the same year\n\n**Conclusion:** The limitation period has been repeatedly interrupted and restarted. Since the last interruption (the most recent collection/negotiation round) occurred before September, and the lawsuit is being prepared in September of the same year, the claim is **well within the limitation period**.\n\n### 5. Practical Considerations\n\n#### Evidence Requirements:\nTo prove interruption of the limitation period, Company A should preserve:\n- The August email from Company B acknowledging the debt\n- All correspondence, emails, meeting records, or other evidence of subsequent collection efforts and negotiations\n- Any written acknowledgments or partial payment promises from Company B\n\n#### Recommended Actions:\n1. **File the lawsuit promptly** in September as planned\n2. **Compile all evidence** of the debt acknowledgment and collection efforts\n3. **Document the timeline** carefully to demonstrate the limitation period has been interrupted\n4. **Consider property preservation measures** (财产保全) if there is concern about Company B transferring assets\n\n---\n\n## Final Answer\n\n### The statute of limitations for Company A's claim is **3 years**, but it has been **interrupted and restarted** multiple times, so the claim remains **fully valid and enforceable**.\n\n**Specifically:**\n\n1. **Base limitation period:** 3 years from the date when the payment obligation became due (30 days after delivery, per Article 188 of the Civil Code)\n\n2. **First interruption:** Company B's August email acknowledging the RMB 500,000 debt and promising to pay \"as soon as possible\" constitutes an agreement to perform the obligation, interrupting the limitation period and restarting the 3-year clock from the date of that email (Article 195(2))\n\n3. **Further interruptions:** Each subsequent collection effort and negotiation round by Company A constitutes a request for performance, further interrupting and restarting the limitation period (Article 195(1))\n\n4. **Current status:** Since the lawyer is preparing to file in September of the same year, and the last interruption occurred during the ongoing negotiations, the lawsuit is **timely filed within the limitation period**\n\n**Legal basis:** Articles 188 and 195 of the Civil Code of the People's Republic of China (中华人民共和国民法典).\n\n---\n\n## Source Reference\n\n- **Law 4** in the repository: 中华人民共和国民法典 (Civil Code of the People's Republic of China), specifically Articles 188, 195, 196, 197, 198, and 199.\n",
